[docs-voice
  [doc [#
docs-src/site/voice.cxd — the docs-voice rules as data (RULED: DOCS-43,
DOCS-47, RS-30).

Two ratchets, each read by a step:

  [python]    scripts/gen_site/docs_voice_check.cx (`make docs-voice-check`,
              TEST_TARGETS). The docs speak for CX on its own identity, and
              name Python only where a reader arriving from it needs the
              bridge (the design's voice rule 4). The step counts the word
              (any case, whole word) in every served SOURCE — the landing
              page, the guide sections and the about page, the reference
              sources, the LLM templates, the dev pages — and holds each
              file to its row here: a file with no row must read ZERO; a file
              with a row may read at most its `allow=`, and when it reads
              LESS the step refuses too, naming the new count, so the number
              here is always the measured one and only ever moves down (a
              ratchet). The three bridge pages keep their rows for good; the
              rows carrying a wave number are the floors DOCS-49's later
              waves lower to zero, one page at a time.

  [fixtures]  scripts/gen_guide/snippet_check.cx (`make guide-snippets-check`).
              Every example on a new or re-voiced page is a [fixture id=]
              citation replayed byte-exact (DOCS-47); `floor=` is the number
              of citations the guide's sections carry — a [fixture] counts
              one, a [pair] counts two — and a wave that lowers it is refused.
              A wave that raises it raises the floor in the same change.
#]]

  [; ── the Python allowances, measured 2026-09-28 on the DOCS-4 wave-1 tree ── ]
  [python
    [; the bridge pages — kept for good (DOCS-43): the migration page, the
       comparison's one section, the quickstart's host-language step ]
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/10-migration.cxd' allow=5 why='the migration page is the bridge for a reader arriving from Python']
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/13-comparison.cxd' allow=13 why='the comparison states the case beside Python in one section (cx-vs-python) and three tooling rows; wave 5 narrows it to the one section']
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/00-quickstart.cxd' allow=8 why='the host-language step (first-binding) shows the four bindings; wave 5 narrows the rest to that step']
    [; the hand-authored about page: the owner writes it, nobody regenerates it ]
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/about.html' allow=3 why='hand-authored by the owner; the page names what CX was measured against']
    [; the PyPI binding's repository page (DOCS-49 wave 4): the word is the
       repository's own NAME — the [repo name=] root the generator requires and
       the file comment that names the page — and the prose names no host
       language; whether a binding page may is the bindings row's open letter ]
    [page path='docs-src/repos/cx-binding-python.cxd' allow=2 why='the repository is named cx-binding-python: its root element and the comment naming the file; the prose names no host language']
    [; floors for waves 5–7 (the voice pass), the count measured now; wave 5
     took 01-intro, 01b-choosing, 03-surfaces and 04-identity to zero and
     wave 5b 02-data-language and 06-code (their rows are gone: a file
     with no row reads zero) ]
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/07-bindings.cxd' allow=62 why='FIXED (RULED: HOSTL-1, L109 = (a)): the subject of the page is the bindings, and a page whose subject is a binding names its host language beside its fixtures; the count is the measured one and never rises']
        [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/11-glossary.cxd' allow=1 wave=7 why='one glossary entry']
    [page path='docs-src/canonical/sections/12-faq.cxd' allow=12 wave=7 why='the FAQ answers questions asked from Python']
